                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "My Review of Humble Abode:\n\nHumble Abode is a good start for Venomyth Game Studio's first game. It's not a very long game but it does have some issues that the developer should probably address. \n\nPros:\n\n-Good attempt at story telling. The game has an aurora of mystery around it (not all of its questions are answered however) so a possible sequel may be in the works. \n\n-The puzzles although simplistic enough do require some forthought. Its not them most challenging puzzles you will ever see though (lots of item searching and such) but the game doesn't overstay its welcome so I can give it a pass.\n\n-Two different ending: One good and the other bad depending on what course of action you take in the end. It doesn't add replayablility but it encourages you to load up your last save and find the other ending.\n\n-The game is a cheap price so you get about two hours of gameplay for a dollar. Not bad!\n\nCons:\n-The game does have some bugs and scripting errors. (Ex. In the guest bedroom, Eddy heres the door open and remarks that the killer may be in the room. After that cutscene, if you do back to that spot the same cutscene will play again even if you already saw it)\n\n-The game lacks a \"objective\" screen for finding out where to go and what to find. Granted Eddy's home is not very big but that could definetly be a useful thing to add in an update since the game require some code entering to continue in spots.\n\n-Depending on how good or bad you are at puzzles, you could potentially get stuck if puzzle solving isnt your thing.\n\nOverall, if you want a thiller that can last you through the afternoon try this game. It may not be for everyone but its fun while it lasts.\n",
